## Project layout

- `src/main.ts` — bootstraps `AppComponent` with `appConfig` (`bootstrapApplication`, no
  NgModule).
- `src/app/app.component.*` — the root standalone component.
- `src/app/app.config.ts` — application-wide providers (change detection, router).
- `src/app/app.routes.ts` — the router config; starts empty. Add lazy standalone routes here.
- `angular.json` — the Angular CLI workspace (`{{ app_name }}` project: build, serve, test,
  lint targets).
- `eslint.config.js` / `.prettierrc.json` — lint and format rules.
- `karma.conf.js` — Karma runs headless Chromium via the `ChromeHeadlessNoSandbox` launcher
  (the container is unprivileged, so the Chromium sandbox is off). `CHROME_BIN` is set in
  the image.

## Running things

- `npm start` — `ng serve` on http://localhost:4200 (port forwarded from the devcontainer).
- `npm run build` — production build into `dist/{{ app_name }}/`.
- `npm test` — Karma/Jasmine unit tests (watch mode); `npm test -- --watch=false` for a
  single run, which is what the pre-commit hook does.
- `npm run lint` — ESLint over `src/**/*.ts` and the templates.
- `npx ng generate component features/foo` (or `service`, `guard`, ...) — scaffold new
  pieces so they follow the workspace conventions.

## Conventions

- Components, directives and pipes are **standalone**; never add an `NgModule`.
- Add dependencies with `npm install` / `npm install --save-dev`; do not hand-edit
  `package.json` dependency ranges.
- Every new component/service in `src/app/` gets a matching `*.spec.ts`; the pre-commit
  hook runs the full Karma suite.
